<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inhambane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inhambane is a port town on a sheltered bay on the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> called there in 1498, and Portuguese traders were active in the 16th century, dealing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and later in slaves. In the 19th century its hinterland was contested with the Gaza kingdom, and the town is now the capital of Inhambane Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inhambane.jpg" alt="Inhambane" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oscar Wagenmans · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o_do_Caminho_de_Ferro_Inhambane_back_side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inhambane, also known as Terra de Boa Gente, 'Land of the Good People', lies on Inhambane Bay, 470 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>. The settlement owes its existence to a deep inlet into which the small Mutamba River flows. Two protective sandy headlands shelter the harbour and form a sandbank. The sister town of Maxixe stands across the bay, and motor and dhow taxis sail between the two. Inhambane has one of the largest working fleets of dhows on the Swahili Coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Inhambane is one of the oldest settlements on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>'s eastern coast. It is recorded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> that submitted to successive powers, beginning with the Kilwa Sultanate, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a>, and finally Portuguese Mozambique. Dhows traded here as early as the 11th century, and Muslim and Persian traders, the first outsiders to arrive by sea, dealt in pearls and ambergris. The area was known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> spinning and production by the Bitonga. Before the Portuguese came, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> had invaded and formed a number of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, dominating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> cotton workers and profiting from the trade with Muslim merchants.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> put in to replenish stocks and explore, and named the place Terra de Boa Gente. In 1505 a ship sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> was wrecked south of the town, and the Portuguese gained a first meeting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> chiefs. They established a permanent trading post in 1534, and in 1560 Inhambane was chosen as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission to Southeast Africa. The port grew as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slave trading centre, particularly in the 18th century under mostly Indian control.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a> destroyed it in 1834, but it grew rapidly in the second half of the century as a town of Portuguese East Africa, and the old cathedral and old mosque were built in that period. In the 20th century its standing declined, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>, called Lourenço Marques before 1975, became the main centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Inhambane is the capital of Inhambane Province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2017 gave it a population of 79,724, up from 54,157 in 1997. The Cathedral of Our Lady of Conception, about 170 years old, stands in the old quarter, and the city has a museum and a central market, the Mercado Central, on its main boulevard. The beaches of Tofo and Barra lie nearby.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inhambane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He conquered Tsonga and Chopi communities and defeated Portuguese posts at Delagoa Bay and <b>Inhambane</b>, building a kingdom that reached the Zambezi and the eastern Zimbabwe highlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Schoemansdal lived mainly from elephant hunting and the ivory trade with Delagoa Bay and <b>Inhambane</b>, using African hunters armed with guns.</blockquote><a href="/state/zoutpansberg-republic">Zoutpansberg Republic</a></li></ul></section>
